<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Linear map with given null space</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Give an example of a linear map \(T:\mathbb{R}^2\to\mathbb{R}^2\) whose null space is spanned by the vector \({@nv@}\).</p>
<p>Enter \(T(x_1,x_2)\) as one formula for each component, using the variables <code>x1</code> and <code>x2</code>.</p>
<div>\(T(x_1,x_2) = \Big(\) [[input:ans1]] \(,\) [[input:ans2]] \(\Big)\)</div>
<div>[[validation:ans1]] [[validation:ans2]]</div>
<div>[[feedback:prt1]]</div>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>The null space of \(T\) must contain \({@nv@}\), so \(T(1,{@av@}) = (0,0)\). It must also contain nothing else, so it is one-dimensional, and \(T\) has rank \(1\).</p>
<p>A linear map \(T:\mathbb{R}^2\to\mathbb{R}^2\) has components \(a x_1 + b x_2\). Each component vanishes at \((1,{@av@})\) exactly when it is a multiple of \({@ta1@}\). So the answers are the maps \(T(x_1,x_2) = (p({@ta1@}),\, q({@ta1@}))\) with \(p\) and \(q\) not both zero, for example \(T(x_1,x_2) = ({@ta1@}, {@ta2@})\).</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>1</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0.1</penalty>
    <hidden>0</hidden>
    <idnumber/>
    <stackversion>
      <text>2026111900</text>
    </stackversion>
    <questionvariables>
      <text>stack_include_contrib("validators.mac");
/* A validator takes one argument, so supply the variables here. */
validate_x2(ex) := validate_vars(ex, [x1, x2]);
X: [x1, x2];
/* The null space is spanned by nv = (1, av). */
av: rand_with_prohib(-3, 3, [0]);
nv: matrix([1], [av]);
/* The simplest answer: both components multiples of av*x1 - x2. */
ta1: av*x1 - x2;
ta2: 0;</text>
    </questionvariables>
